# env: PATH=${MNML_SHIMS}/ai:${PATH}
# The strip cluster's Claude mark. It used to pale while no session
# ran and light up when one did, so a chip sitting beside four buttons
# that never change read as disabled rather than as idle. It is
# Anthropic's orange, always — whether a session is live is the
# SESSIONS panel's to show, and the statusline's.
#
# `tools/shims/ai/claude` stands in for the CLI (it sleeps), so the
# chip shows without the real binary and no transcript reaches the
# screen. The mark sits at column 106 of the strip, before the
# terminal and the three split buttons.
write a.txt "alpha alpha"
open a.txt
expect screen contains "󱸀"
expect color 106 1 fg #d97757
# A click is the way to the sessions: the SESSIONS section comes up,
# and with nothing running it starts one — the command the panel's own
# `+ New session` row runs.
click 106 1
wait 300
expect screen contains "SESSIONS"
expect pane claude
# The mark did not change colour under the click, and it is still
# orange with a session live.
expect color 106 1 fg #d97757
# A second click shows the panel again and does NOT open a second
# session: the chip is a way in, not one more launcher.
command view.activity_explorer
expect screen lacks "SESSIONS"
click 106 1
wait 300
expect screen contains "SESSIONS"
# The report behind this: "when I click the claude code icon it lights
# up blue afterwards". It was never the cluster's mark — that one is
# fixed above at every step. It is the TAB the click opened: a Claude
# pane's glyph wears its accent (`ui/accent_color`), and the first
# Claude session's accent is Claude's orange — the same #d97757 the
# cluster's mark wears (`ui.accent_defaults.claude`,
# `pane_accent.defaultFor`); a second session would take the first
# free ladder slot after `a.txt`'s editor's green, blue. Before this
# change a click opened a session every time, so a click with one
# already up put a differently-marked tab on the strip under the icon.
expect color 77 1 fg #d97757
